{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat /Users/johnlee/.local/share/wt-utils/worktrees/73e0/stamped-in-practice/assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/share/wt-utils/worktrees/73e0/stamped-in-practice/assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/verdicts.md\n'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat /Users/johnlee/.local/share/wt-utils/worktrees/73e0/stamped-in-practice/assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md\ncat /Users/johnlee/.local/share/wt-utils/worktrees/73e0/stamped-in-practice/assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/verdicts.md\n'","aggregated_output":"# Response, report and stamp\n\nEvery STAMPED assessment produces four files (five with the optional fresh-rerun report), named for **the day the assessment was made** (UTC, `YYYY-MM-DD`) — not the object's commit date, and not the date of an earlier assessment being redone:\n\n| File | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `<date>-assessment.md` | the human report (layout below) |\n| `<date>-response.json` | the machine-readable per-item answer, with evidence and pins |\n| `<date>-probes.json` | what the scripted probes said, unedited, including the ones overridden |\n| `<date>-url.txt` | the checklist-app URL, on one line |\n| `<date>-fresh-rerun.json` | only when the expensive rerun probe was actually run |\n\nThe URL is derived from the response, never maintained by hand.\nTwo assessments of the same object on the same day get a `-2` suffix on all four; the second one's report says what changed and why.\nWhere the files are written is the calling skill's business; their names and shapes are this file's.\n\n## Response JSON\n\nA superset of the web app's persistence format 3.\nThe app ignores keys it does not know, so one file serves both.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"checklist_version\": \"0.3.1\",\n  \"responses\": {\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/009\": {\n      \"value\": \"no\",\n      \"reason\": \"code/plot.py line 12 opens an absolute path under a user home directory.\",\n      \"status\": \"unmet\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"uri\": \"code/plot.py#L12\", \"description\": \"open(<absolute path under $HOME>)\", \"sha256\": null}\n      ],\n      \"source\": \"probe:no-abs-paths\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    }\n  },\n  \"schema_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"principles_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"checklist_source\": {\n    \"repo\": \"https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-checklist-schema\",\n    \"tag\": \"v0.3.1\",\n    \"commit\": \"af259bad237fa57b856417db8dba45a1b283a527\"\n  },\n  \"extra_items\": [\n    {\"key\": \"licence-declared\", \"status\": \"unmet\", \"reason\": \"No LICENSE, LICENSES/ or REUSE.toml; paper S.2/D.3 have no checklist item.\", \"evidence\": []}\n  ],\n  \"object\": {\"url\": \"…\", \"commit\": \"…\", \"captured_at\": \"2026-10-13T12:00:00Z\", \"kind\": \"study\"},\n  \"assessor\": {\"skill\": \"assess-alignment-with-stamped-principles\", \"version\": \"0.2.0\", \"harness\": \"claude-code 2.1.x\", \"model\": \"…\", \"date\": \"2026-10-13\",\n               \"dependencies\": [\"stamped-conventions@0.2.0\"]},\n  \"scope\": \"Assessed the repository at the commit above; container images were not pulled.\"\n}\n```\n\n- **Required core** (what the app reads): `checklist_version`, and `responses` with a `value` and a string `reason` per id — *in the payload the encoder builds*. The response JSON you write carries `status`, and `encode` derives `value` from it; `validate` therefore does not ask for a `value` and `set` does not write one. A `value` you write by hand that disagrees with its `status` is an error `validate` reports.\n- Every one of the 31 pinned ids appears exactly once.\n  A missing id is not \"no answer\"; it is a bug the validator catches.\n- `status` is the richer verdict from [`verdicts.md`](verdicts.md); `value` is derived from it, never set by hand.\n- **Cite a line only if you opened it.** `path#L12` is a promise that line 12 says what the reason claims; a run that cited line numbers it had not read then had to strip them all. Without that, cite the file (or `path#section`), which is still a citation a reader can follow.\n- `evidence[]` uses stamped-skills-assessment's `Evidence` shape: `{uri, description, sha256?}`.\n  `uri` is relative to the object's root, or absolute for anything outside it.\n  **The argument lives here, not in `reason`.** 250 characters does not hold two cited accounts plus \"the probe's heuristic said X but Y is true\", and an override needs all three. Make `reason` the one-line verdict a reader sees in the app, and put each account, each command you ran and each number in an `evidence[]` entry with its own `description`.\n- `source` is `probe:<name>`, `judgement`, or `probe:<name>+judgement` when a reading of the object overrode a probe (and then the item is flagged).\n  Several probes may be named — `probe:entry-point+probe:ci-present+judgement` — which is what a verdict reached by **comparing two probes' evidence** looks like, and that comparison is itself the finding often enough to be worth recording.\n- `flags[]` is optional and holds what a reader has to know about *how* the answer was reached, not what it says:\n  - `mapping-questioned` — the probe answered this item from a property the item does not ask about ([`verdicts.md`](verdicts.md) rule 10);\n  - `probe-overridden` — a reading of the object changed the probe's verdict;\n  - `unverified-claim` — the verdict rests on something not confirmed independently, and the report says so;\n  - `upstream-duplicate` — the checklist asks this question twice (must/014 and should/008 are the same sentence), so this answer repeats another by design rather than by mistake.\n\n  Flagged items get their own short list in the report, after the per-item table.\n  The list is **open**: these four are the ones in use, and a consumer that meets an unfamiliar flag shows it rather than dropping it.\n- The `assessor` block may also carry `drafted_by` (the script or step that produced a draft, where that differs from the skill — `scripts/run_probes.py` sets it) and `note` (anything a reader needs about the assessor itself rather than about the object). Both are provenance *within* the assessor, not a second assessor.\n- `extra_items[]` carries findings with no checklist id (licences, T.4) so they are reported without bending an existing id.\n- `object.commit` names the inspected repository or preservation revision, not necessarily the worker's delivered state.\n  Identify the original delivery snapshot/hash, capture omissions and stop-state evidence in `scope` and evidence citations; distinguish supplied resources and coordinator additions so they cannot earn worker credit. Keep the actual Git commit whenever one exists. For a delivery without a commit, set `object.commit` to `null` and supply `object.capture` with a retained manifest/archive's `uri` and SHA-256 `sha256`; never substitute the capture hash for a Git commit.\n  The manifest must identify the delivered paths and content hashes plus capture omissions; an archive must contain the assessed bytes.\n  Retain the referenced file so another assessor can verify the hash and boundary.\n  Use `encode_response.py template -o <date>-response.json` when no Git probe draft is available, fill the assessment metadata and answers, then run `encode_response.py stamp <date>-response.json --capture <manifest-or-archive>` to hash the actual file.\n  This preserves an existing commit; record the capture time in `object.captured_at` and the boundary in `scope`.\n  Validation checks the record's shape; independently verify the manifest against the delivery.\n\n`validate` checks the complete record against [`response-schema.json`](response-schema.json), using `jsonschema`, then checks checklist pins and verdict semantics. Stamp the response before final validation. The metadata is required for a completed assessment, even though the web app itself reads only the compact payload.\n\n## The provenance stamp\n\n`assessor` plus `object` plus `checklist_source` **is** the stamp, and every report repeats it in prose at its foot:\n\n> Produced by `assess-alignment-with-stamped-principles` 0.1.0 with `stamped-conventions` 0.1.0, checklist 0.3.1 (`stamped-checklist-schema@v0.3.1`, `af259ba`), on <object url> at commit `<sha>`, 2026-10-13, <harness>/<model>.\n> Probe output: `<path to the probe JSON>`.\n\nWhen no commit exists, replace `at commit <sha>` with `at capture <uri>, SHA-256 <sha256>` in the prose stamp.\n\nA report without the stamp cannot be compared against another iteration, so it is not finished.\n\n## Report layout\n\nUse this order, so the reader meets the actionable part first:\n\n1. **Verdict line** — `MUST: n met / n partial / n unmet / n not-checked`, then SHOULD and MAY, each with its denominator.\n   No single overall score, ever.\n2. **What to fix first** — at most five, highest leverage first, each as *what to change* → *why it matters to this project* → *the item ids it closes*.\n3. **What the project already does well** — two or three, with citations.\n4. **Per-item table** — MUST first, then SHOULD, then MAY: id, principle codes, status, evidence, reason.\n   `scripts/encode_response.py table <response>.json` renders exactly this from the response; it is not a table to type by hand.\n5. **Flagged items** — every item carrying a `flags` entry, with one line each: what the probe measured versus what the item asks, or what the override was.\n   An empty list is worth stating as \"none\".\n6. **Not checked** — each with what would settle it.\n7. **Goals and kind** — the project's stated goals (and where they came from: documentation, or the user's answers), its kind, and the emphasis that follows.\n8. **The checklist URL**, the stamp, and a pointer to the response JSON.\n   The URL is ~8–10 kB of base64 for a full 31-item response, so the report **links** `<date>-url.txt` rather than inlining it; a link a reader can click, not a wall they have to scroll.\n\n## The checklist-app URL\n\nFacts, from [stamped-checklist](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-checklist) at `d24d7ca`:\n\n- `PERSISTENCE_FORMAT = 3` (`src/persistence.js`).\n- The `responses` parameter is **standard base64** (not URL-safe) of UTF-8 JSON `{\"checklist_version\", \"responses\"}`, then percent-encoded as a query parameter.\n- The app builds `?checklist=<v>&responses_version=<v>&cols=auto&sections=off&format=3&responses=<b64>` (`src/script.js`, `syncPersistentURL`).\n- It accepts `value ∈ {\"yes\",\"no\",null}`, requires `reason` to be a **string** (empty is fine), truncates it to 250 characters, shows it only for `\"no\"`, drops keys it does not know, and **rejects the whole load** on an id that is not in the requested checklist version.\n\n`scripts/encode_response.py` is the only writer of that URL, and `decode` is the reverse — that is how a checklist a person filled in the web app becomes reference answers for calibration, with no extra labelling tool.\n\n## When the full assessment record matters\n\nThe response JSON is the canonical assessment record. When a calling evaluation harness supplies pinned export schemas, derive its additional records from that JSON; do not invent formats or make an absent exporter block the assessment.\n# Verdict vocabulary\n\nOne status per checklist item, one reason, and evidence for anything other than `not-applicable`.\nThis reconciles @asmacdo's draft (pass/partial/fail/unknown), STAMPED-dl's met/partial/unmet/restricted/n-a, the [K-Dense #265](https://github.com/K-Dense-AI/scientific-agent-skills/pull/265) review (\"not checked with the tooling available\" is not \"unmet\"), and the judgement statuses of [stamped-skills-assessment](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-skills-assessment).\n\n| `status` | Use it when | App `value` | App `reason` holds |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `met` | Cited evidence shows the property holds across the object | `\"yes\"` | Not shown by the app; the citation lives in the response JSON |\n| `partial` | It holds by design but is violated somewhere specific, or holds in some components only | `\"no\"` | Where it falls short, concretely |\n| `unmet` | Evidence shows the property is absent | `\"no\"` | The shortest fix |\n| `not-checked` | You could not check: tool missing, cost, access, or out of the agreed scope | `null` | Why, and what would settle it |\n| `not-applicable` | The property cannot apply to this kind of object | `null` | `N/A: <reason>` |\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **A MUST item is never satisfied by `partial`.**\n   Report `partial` and count it as a MUST failure.\n2. **`not-checked` is reported separately from `unmet`, always.**\n   Never fold it in, never score it as zero, and never let it disappear from a denominator: say `14/17 MUST met, 2 unmet, 1 not-checked`.\n3. **`met` requires a citation** a reader can open: a path (with a line number where it helps), a commit, a run record, a URL, or a command with its output.\n   \"The project uses DataLad\" is not evidence; `.datalad/config` at commit `abc1234` is.\n4. **`unmet` requires evidence of absence**, not failure to find evidence.\n   If you only failed to find it, that is `not-checked`.\n5. **`not-applicable` is rare and argued**, and there are exactly two grounds for it:\n   - **the kind of object** ([`project-kinds.md`](project-kinds.md)) — an object with no computation at all can be `not-applicable` on E.1; a dataset still has an environment for whatever produced it;\n   - **an empty subject** — the thing the item asks about does not exist here.\n     should/009 (\"are restricted data or software kept in their own modules?\") on an object whose every component is public has no subject: `met` would be a citation-free claim about a component that does not exist, and `unmet` would be false.\n\n   Either way the reason names the ground, starting with `N/A:`.\n   An item you merely could not check is `not-checked`, never this.\n   After inspecting the complete delivery, use `not-applicable` for must/005 when no large binary data exists; apply the may/001 subject boundary below.\n\n   **Not** an N/A ground: *there was nothing of this kind to do*.\n   An object with no host assumptions to document satisfies must/011 — the requirement is \"write down your assumptions\", and having none meets it.\n   Answer `met`, and let the reason carry the emptiness (\"no host assumptions beyond a POSIX shell and the tracked lockfile; nothing undocumented remains\").\n   The difference from an empty subject is whether the *requirement* can be satisfied vacuously (must/011: yes) or the *property* has no instance to hold of (should/009 on an all-public object: no).\n\n   **A declared process that has never run is `partial`**, not `met` and not `not-checked`.\n   must/014 and should/008 ask for a process *and* for its updates to be tracked; a documented regeneration command with no update in the history is half of that, so say \"declared in <file>, no instance in the history yet\".\n   **The commit that introduced the file is not an instance of updating it.**\n   A lockfile committed once, with a regeneration command in its header, is \"declared, never run\": the creation is the declaration, not an instance of the process.\n   **A declared process with instances in the history is `met`**: cite one.\n   A blind run stalled on exactly this gap — a re-install step the history shows was carried out seventeen times fits neither \"never run\" nor \"unmet\" — so the three cases are: instances in the history → `met`; declared, no instance → `partial`; not declared → `unmet`.\n   **The bar for \"declared\" is a command a reader could run**: `# pinned with uv pip compile code/requirements.in` in a lockfile header clears it; \"dependencies are kept up to date\" does not, and that is `unmet`.\n   This is the commonest borderline case, and it comes up on every young object.\n6. **`restricted`** (from STAMPED-dl) is **not a status here**.\n   Restricted data that is properly isolated is `met` on should/009 and `not-checked` elsewhere with \"access-restricted\" as the reason.\n7. **Equivalent evidence counts.**\n   Name the mechanism you found rather than the tool you expected.\n   Preserve the checklist pin; these are explicit property-based interpretations, not edits to its wording:\n   - **must/003:** an installable package declaration in a delivered environment specification counts as inclusion; physical vendoring is not required. Judge completeness under must/010, exact environment identity under must/015, and archived artifacts under should/007; missing version pins alone do not show incompleteness.\n   - **must/007:** discoverable installation and usage instructions can satisfy the item under another filename or in an equivalent delivered interface; `README.md` and `Makefile` are not mandatory names.\n   - **may/001:** assess independently maintained project components, not ordinary runtime or package dependencies. No such components → `not-applicable`; otherwise require independently identified, retrievable links with clear boundaries, not necessarily Git submodules. An unidentified copy or unresolvable path fails. Judge missing or incomplete dependency environments under must/003 and must/010; they do not turn packages into project modules.\n   - **must/006:** execution evidence must link the actual results and actions to their environment; a recognized provenance format or tool name alone is neither necessary nor sufficient.\n   - **should/002, should/006:** inspect equivalent repeated-testing and environment-reconstitution records, including manual or scheduled runs; missing CI configuration alone proves neither property absent.\n   Apply these interpretations consistently within a comparison and name any equivalent evidence used.\n8. **Scripted before judged.**\n   Run applicable probes and cite their output, then inspect evidence outside their supported representations.\n   A tracked-path inventory, recognized filename or parsed DataLad record is not a complete delivered-artifact or provenance inventory.\n   Missing probe support is `not-checked` pending inspection, not evidence of absence; record which probe or reading produced each verdict.\n9. **Disagreements are surfaced, not averaged.**\n   When a probe and a reading of the object disagree, report both and flag the item for the human.\n10. **When the object tells two stories about how it runs, that disagreement *is* the answer.**\n    An object can carry three accounts of its own execution — what the documentation says, what CI exercises, and what the run records recorded — and they need not agree.\n    Judge **A.1 items (must/007, must/008, must/017) on the documented route**, because that is what a reader will follow; judge **T items on the records**, because that is what produced the results.\n    Where the accounts disagree, the verdict is at most `partial`, the reason names both accounts, and the disagreement is reported as a finding in its own right: a README promising a container that the `Makefile` never invokes is worse than either an honest README or an honest Makefile.\n    Four pairings and where each lands: documentation against the mechanism → **must/007, must/008, must/017**, and **should/002**, because a workflow tested against instructions nobody can follow is not tested; the mechanism against the run records → **must/006 and must/008** (what produced the results is not what is offered to reproduce them); CI against the records → **should/006** (the environment being tested is not the one the results came from); and the object's *metadata* against its own layout — a `dataset_description.json` naming a pipeline the history never ran — which has no checklist item and goes in `extra_items`.\n    If naming both accounts will not fit in 250 characters, name the worse one in the reason and put the other in `evidence`; do not drop it.\n\n    **An accurate disclosure is not a disagreement.**\n    This rule exists for accounts that conflict *silently* — a README promising a container the `Makefile` never invokes.\n    An object whose documentation states the difference and says which route produced the results is telling one story in two parts, and the items above stay `met` on it: the test is whether a reader following the documentation would be misled, not whether two routes exist.\n    Two runs in a row marked a disclosed, accurate two-route object `partial` on four items under this rule; that is this rule being over-applied, and the reason to say so here.\n    **The disclosure has to be true.**\n    A comment in a CI workflow claiming it exercises the recorded environment, in a job whose steps never touch it, is not a disclosure: it is the silent conflict with an extra sentence, and it is worse than silence because it tells a reader not to check.\n11. **A right answer reached by the wrong route is still a finding.**\n    When a probe answers an item from a property the item does not ask about, the verdict may happen to be right and the reasoning is still wrong.\n    Record it in the item's `flags` (`mapping-questioned`), say which property the probe actually measured, and keep your own verdict with `source` ending in `+judgement`.\n    That is how a wrong probe-to-item mapping gets fixed instead of being inherited by every later report.\n\n## Mapping to the app\n\n`scripts/encode_response.py` does this mapping; do not hand-roll it.\nIt also enforces the app's constraints: `value ∈ {\"yes\",\"no\",null}`, `reason` a string of at most 250 characters, and every id known to the pinned checklist.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /tmp/build_assessment.py <<'PY'\nimport json, os, hashlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nout=Path(os.environ['STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT'])\nbase=json.load(open(out/'response.json'))\nresponses=base['responses']\ncap='62844be8e123ee4970d90409a641082a3126743ee3b90d914b2b5ef26a50aaf1'\n\ndef ev(path, desc, sha=None):\n    return {'uri':f'capture://{cap}/content/workspace/{path}', 'description':desc, 'sha256':sha}\ndef setitem(i,status,reason,evidence,source='judgement',flags=None):\n    responses[f'stamped-checklist:{i}']={'reason':reason,'status':status,'evidence':evidence,'source':source,'flags':flags or []}\n# evidence shortcuts\nrep=ev('REPORT.md','Worker report at stop state; original capture SHA-256 '+cap,'87a86bc213b9e8157957666a1263c32249e4ff4ae5d2a677a115aaf89c743248')\nscript=ev('analyze.py','Worker analysis script at stop state; original capture SHA-256 '+cap,'abb28e87b1d03c0d7bcd8effac1bb9afa785fe5004a44ff7e31d017e91790dac')\nparts=ev('input/participants.tsv','Cohort metadata; original capture SHA-256 '+cap,'faca815bcb042144bdb8dcff915f45de0d27650f60416ed76a5a72557341abbf')\nmethods=ev('input/upstream-methods.md','Fixed upstream model description; original capture SHA-256 '+cap,'508d832c8787ed8303c3de101c2c76f40a3aa5dec53e6993dc2a49c4b7bcf90b')\nprobe= {'uri':'probes.json','description':'Frozen runner probe output; saved assessment artifact','sha256':hashlib.sha256((out/'probes.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest()}\nreceipt={'uri':os.environ['STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT'],'description':'Worker stop receipt; SHA-256 cac1a272faf44f4e459f21af394256880b4c98e91e94a44b6939cc92cba45c83','sha256':'cac1a272faf44f4e459f21af394256880b4c98e91e94a44b6939cc92cba45c83'}\nsetitem('must/001','met','All captured workspace content is under the workspace root.',[rep])\nsetitem('must/002','met','All 54 input-map links resolve within the captured workspace to retained annex objects.',[ev('input/data/','Participant-map links are internal workspace paths; the complete capture retains their annex content.'),ev('.git/annex/objects/','Retained annex object bytes are inside the common root.')])\nsetitem('must/003','unmet','No delivered environment specification or vendored external software dependencies are present.',[rep,probe])\nsetitem('must/004','unmet','Git is used, but the report, analysis, cache and results were untracked at worker stop.',[{'uri':'git status --short --ignored (operational copy)','description':'Probe observed ?? REPORT.md, ?? analyze.py, ?? atlas_cache/, ?? results/; worker stop receipt records the same stop state.','sha256':None},receipt,probe])\nsetitem('must/005','met','The 54 large participant maps are tracked as annex pointers with retained annex content.',[ev('.git/annex/','Captured annex object store and 54 input links.'),probe])\nsetitem('must/006','unmet','The worker report names a Python executable and packages but does not capture or link an exact environment to the result run.',[rep,probe,receipt])\nsetitem('must/007','unmet','The report gives a reproduction command but no installation instructions or complete dependency setup.',[rep,probe],source='probe:entry-point+judgement',flags=['probe-overridden'])\nsetitem('must/008','met','The report clearly names analyze.py and gives a command to reproduce the outputs; the script and outputs were present at stop.',[rep,script,ev('results/summary.json','Reported numeric output exists in the worker stop delivery.')],source='probe:entry-point+judgement',flags=['probe-overridden'])\nsetitem('must/009','unmet','The documented reproduction command embeds a host-specific absolute interpreter path.',[ev('REPORT.md','Line 43 gives an absolute path to the common-environment Python binary.','87a86bc213b9e8157957666a1263c32249e4ff4ae5d2a677a115aaf89c743248'),probe],source='probe:no-abs-paths+judgement',flags=['probe-overridden'])\nsetitem('must/010','unmet','No dependency declaration specifies all required software; the report lists package names only.',[rep,probe])\nsetitem('must/011','unmet','The only run route depends on a machine-specific interpreter path without documenting its host assumptions.',[rep])\nsetitem('must/012','unmet','The report lists Python packages but gives no system requirements, versions or installation route.',[rep])\nsetitem('must/013','unmet','No environment specification is included in the captured project or worker-tracked state.',[probe,rep])\nsetitem('must/014','unmet','No dependency-update process or tracked environment specification is declared.',[probe,rep])\nsetitem('must/015','unmet','No pinned environment manifest or container digest is supplied for exact environment recreation.',[probe,rep])\nsetitem('must/016','unmet','No worker-supplied environment specification is available to share and use.',[probe,rep])\nsetitem('must/017','unmet','The report gives a command but no instructions for obtaining or setting up its environment.',[rep])\nsetitem('should/001','met','Git and git-annex are used for project files and large input maps.',[probe,ev('.git/annex/','Annex-backed input maps.')])\nsetitem('should/002','not-checked','No CI configuration is present; equivalent repeated-testing history was not independently established. Inspect worker-run history to settle this.',[probe])\nsetitem('should/003','partial','Inputs and results have separate folders, but code and environment are not separated into modules.',[ev('input/','Input data and metadata folder.'),script,ev('results/','Separate derived-output folder.')])\nsetitem('should/009','not-applicable','N/A: no restricted data or software is identified in this public-input group-analysis delivery.',[ev('input/sources.json','Sources identify the supplied public study inputs.')])\nsetitem('should/004','unmet','The worker stop record supplies no evidence that the workflow was tested in a fresh container, batch job, clean VM or cloud instance.',[receipt,probe])\nsetitem('should/005','unmet','No disposable or isolated execution environment is declared or supplied.',[rep,probe])\nsetitem('should/006','not-checked','No worker environment specification is supplied; equivalent repeated environment-reconstitution history was not independently established.',[probe])\nsetitem('should/007','unmet','The delivered atlas cache is archived, but no executable environment artifact or dependency environment is supplied.',[ev('atlas_cache/README.md','Captured dataset cache exists.'),probe])\nsetitem('should/008','unmet','No environment specification or update process is declared.',[rep,probe])\nsetitem('may/001','not-applicable','N/A: the delivery has no independently maintained project components; input maps are captured annex data and runtime packages are dependencies.',[ev('input/data/','Captured participant maps are annex-backed data, not linked project components.'),probe])\nsetitem('may/002','unmet','The report lists key files but does not document module boundaries and how components relate or recombine.',[rep])\nsetitem('may/003','partial','Input and results paths are distinct, but no explicit modular boundary or interface makes independent updates safe.',[script,ev('input/','Input module directory.'),ev('results/','Results directory.')])\nsetitem('may/004','not-applicable','N/A: no composed project modules or independently maintained components are present.',[probe,rep])\nsetitem('may/005','met','The report provides a reproducibility file list that helps readers navigate the script and outputs.',[ev('REPORT.md','Reproducibility files section, lines 31–44.','87a86bc213b9e8157957666a1263c32249e4ff4ae5d2a677a115aaf89c743248')])\nbase['scope']='Independent assessment of the complete original stopped delivery using the operational copy; original capture SHA-256 62844be8e123ee4970d90409a641082a3126743ee3b90d914b2b5ef26a50aaf1, original worker HEAD 8a84ddc3eec68d0b7513f9a15ce37174288b36b7. Worker-stop state controls practice attribution; later coordinator preservation is excluded. Probes and one documented complete-delivery replay were run. Assessment output is outside the target.'\nbase['assessor'].update({'skill':'assess-alignment-with-stamped-principles','version':'0.1.0','harness':'Codex API','model':'GPT-6','date':'2026-10-16','drafted_by':'independent judgement with frozen probes'})\nbase['object'].update({'url':None,'commit':'8a84ddc3eec68d0b7513f9a15ce37174288b36b7','captured_at':'2026-10-16T06:59:04.514702+00:00','kind':'study'})\nbase['extra_items']=[\n {'key':'license-declared','status':'unmet','reason':'The complete worker delivery declares no reuse license; coordinator capture availability is not a worker publication route.','evidence':[probe,ev('input/sources.json','Source metadata does not declare a project reuse license.')]}]\n(out/'response.json').write_text(json.dumps(base,indent=2)+'\\\\n')\n\n# Human report, including scientific judgment and item counts.\nreport='''# Independent assessment: NARPS Hypothesis 1\n\n**STAMPED MUST:** 4 met / 0 partial / 13 unmet / 0 not-checked (17 items). **SHOULD:** 1 met / 2 partial / 4 unmet / 2 not-checked / 1 not-applicable (10 items). **MAY:** 1 met / 1 partial / 2 unmet / 0 not-checked / 2 not-applicable (6 items). These are item findings, not an overall score.\n\n## Scientific assessment\n\n**Scientific adequacy: supported.** The reported conclusion is well supported as a statement that this analysis did not establish a positive vmPFC gain effect. It uses the complete 54-person equal-indifference cohort, performs whole-brain maximum-T FWE correction, names a reproducible bilateral medial orbitofrontal proxy, and separates descriptive ROI uncertainty from corrected inference. The reported mean in that proxy is −0.008 supplied units (95% t interval −0.035 to 0.019), with no corrected voxels in the proxy. It correctly describes the interval as non-equivalence evidence and avoids inferring absence of any neural association. All ten selected outputs were recreated byte-for-byte in the single replay (see \"'`replay/replay-result.json`).\n\n**Reported hypothesis outcome (verbatim):** “In the 54-person NARPS equal-indifference group, the supplied gain-effect maps do **not** support a positive gain association in vmPFC under whole-brain family-wise error (FWE) correction.” [Original capture `content/workspace/REPORT.md`, SHA-256 `87a86bc213b9e8157957666a1263c32249e4ff4ae5d2a677a115aaf89c743248`.] Numeric support: `content/workspace/results/summary.json` reports n=54, 192,339 analysis voxels, 23 corrected voxels outside the proxy, vmPFC proxy mean −0.00795, descriptive 95% t interval [−0.03455, 0.01864], and 0 corrected proxy voxels (SHA-256 `a49299fd75aa0baa130921e207d3e14aa5ca0dd3e7063e2d31203e45219d5e06`).\n\nThe model is appropriate for independent participant-level effects when variance maps are unavailable. Sign-flip symmetry is an explicit assumption; the mask, finite/nonzero coverage rule and common image geometry are inspectable in `content/workspace/analyze.py` (SHA-256 `abb28e87b1d03c0d7bcd8effac1bb9afa785fe5004a44ff7e31d017e91790dac`). The report appropriately treats AAL3 medial orbitofrontal cortex as a proxy, notes template differences and gain regressor scaling, and does not interpret posterior-inferior corrected foci as vmPFC. A limitation is that the reported t interval is descriptive and not a whole-brain or equivalence interval; its role is clearly labeled. No material inference error was identified.\n\n## Delivery, attribution and scope\n\nThe canonical original capture is SHA-256 `62844be8e123ee4970d90409a641082a3126743ee3b90d914b2b5ef26a50aaf1`; the original captured HEAD is `8a84ddc3eec68d0b7513f9a15ce37174288b36b7`. Primary file citations in this report are relative to that captured workspace and name each file'\"'s capture SHA-256. The complete capture has 1,209 entries, no declared omissions or capture errors, and preserves ignored files, workspace Git metadata, annex objects and links. The worker-stop receipt (\"'`worker-end-state.json`, SHA-256 `cac1a272faf44f4e459f21af394256880b4c98e91e94a44b6939cc92cba45c83`) records normal worker exit and that the submitted script, report, cache and results were untracked at stop. The scientific artifacts are assessed as supplied; they were not reconstructed from HEAD. Coordinator preservation does not earn worker credit.\n\n**Replay declaration and result.** Before replay, the declared entrypoint was `/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/common-environment/.pixi/envs/default/bin/python analyze.py`, as written in the report. Prerequisites were the captured source, the named interpreter and available Python packages, and the supplied atlas cache; no source repair or environment additions were made. Only the ten generated files listed in `replay-plan.json` were selected for removal and comparison; inputs, participant metadata and source code were protected. The frozen helper made a separate complete-delivery copy from the canonical capture, imposed its 7,200-second limit, and performed no input prefetch. All ten outputs were identical; there were no numerical or geometry differences. The helper'\"'s return code was 0 and elapsed time was 10.96 seconds. There was no assessor malfunction.\n\n**Integrity observation.** The runner's final replay record reports no capture issues. The operational-copy manifest was also compared to the initial operational manifest; any annex keys database metadata change is reported separately in \"'`integrity-check.json`. The operational copy is an observation surface, not worker evidence. No source repair was performed.\n\n**Access.** No worker-declared repository, archive, URL or other authorized retrieval route was identified in the report or Git remote configuration. Therefore no intended recipient route, artifact-coverage test or host-scope test could be established. The coordinator-provided copy is not evidence of worker publication. No access route was tested.\n\n**Exposure.** No assistance-related identifier was used as grading evidence. The report'\"'s external scholarly citations were considered only as cited scientific context.\n\n## STAMPED checklist findings\n\nProbe results are retained in \"'`probes.json`. The entry-point, absolute-path and tracked-tree probe results were interpreted against the complete stop-state files: the report documents the script entrypoint but omits installation instructions; its script and results were untracked; and the reported command embeds an absolute host path. These overrides are flagged in the response. `must/008` is met because the report does provide a clear starting command despite the probe'\"'s tracked-file heuristic.\n\n'''\n(out/'assessment-report.md').write_text(report)\nPY\nSTAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT=\\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT\" STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT=\"$STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT\" python /tmp/build_assessment.py\n'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
